test_guard — PROT_NONE guard VMA kills on access
================================================

Phase D.2 added a cross-arch PROT_NONE VMA at
`[0x5FFFF000, 0x60000000)` (one page below the user carve-out) so
that any wild-pointer read/write below the program's text region
faults into `vma_handle_fault`, sees `prot == 0`, returns -1, and
the arch fault handler kills the process. This replaces the x86
`PT[0xCB]` literal guard that used to live in `vm_create_process_pt`.

The test pattern is fork + access + waitpid: the child writes one
byte to `0x5FFFF000` and dies; the parent waitpids and verifies a
non-zero exit code (the `kernel_kill_process` path sets
`exit_code = -1`). Without the PROT_NONE VMA the access would
either succeed silently (writing into a freshly-allocate-on-write
page, master-roadmap bug) or trigger an opaque kernel fault that
doesn't propagate as an exit-code; either way the test would
print `guard: bad`.

Pass criterion: prints `guard: ok`.

    module oskit.bin.test_guard

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with, syscall6, pm_waitpid}


    const SYS_FORK = 91


    print_idec(v: int)
        var n = v
        if n < 0
            puts("-")
            n = -n
        if n >= 10
            print_idec(n / 10)
        var b: [1]byte
        b[0] = byte('0' + (n % 10))
        puts(string(&b[0], 1))


    fail(code: int)
        puts("guard: bad ")
        print_idec(code)
        puts("\n")
        exit_with(code)


    do_fork() -> int
        int(syscall6(SYS_FORK, i64(0), i64(0),
                     i64(0), i64(0), i64(0), i64(0)))


    main(argv: []string)
        val pid = do_fork()
        if pid < 0
            fail(1)
            return

        if pid == 0

The child writes one byte to the guard page.  The store traps
into the arch fault handler, `kernel_handle_user_fault` calls
`vma_handle_fault` which sees `prot == 0` and returns -1, and
the kernel kills the process with `exit_code = -1`.  The line
below is unreachable in a correctly-Guarded kernel.  If we DO
reach it, `exit_with(99)` flags a regression — the parent's
`pm_waitpid` will see 99 instead of -1 and the test will report
`guard: bad`.

            var p: *byte = *byte(u64(0x5FFFF000))
            *p = byte(0xAA)

            exit_with(99)
            return

Parent path.  `pm_waitpid` returns the child's exit_code; -1 means
the kernel killed it (the path we want), 99 means the child
unexpectedly survived its write to the guard region.

        val rc = pm_waitpid(pid)
        if rc != -1
            fail(2)
            return

        puts("guard: ok\n")
        exit()
